  "id": "Compound::DB00195 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Betaxolol is implicated in the regulation of Adrb1 through a series of interconnected pathways that involve beta-adrenergic antagonism. The evidence suggests that Betaxolol, through its interaction with Levobunolol, binds to Adrb1, thereby modulating its activity. This mechanism is consistent across multiple paths, where Betaxolol is linked to a decrease in corneal sensitivity, Peyronie's disease, and hypoaesthesia eye, all of which are associated with Levobunolol's effects. The shared involvement of Adrb1 in these pathways, along with the common ancestor of Betaxolol and Levobunolol as a beta-adrenergic antagonist, indicates a broader role for this class of compounds in influencing adrenergic signaling. The integration of these findings highlights a potential mechanism by which Betaxolol exerts its effects on Adrb1, emphasizing the importance of beta-adrenergic receptor modulation in the pathophysiology of these conditions.",
